re rather than chemical pigments to create brilliant colors.","concept_ids":[],"chapter_title":"Structural Color and Optical Surfaces"},{"type":"section","course":"bio-inspired","chapter":"06","section":"01","title":"Module 6: Thermal Regulation & Architecture","description":"Analyze how termite mounds use thermal mass and pressure-driven airflow, then translate those principles into passive architectural cooling strategies.","concept_ids":["stack_effect","thermal_mass","passive_cooling","biomimetic_architecture"],"chapter_title":"Thermal Regulation and Environmental Control"},{"type":"section","course":"bio-inspired","chapter":"07","section":"01","title":"Module 7: Self-Healing Materials","description":"Examine how biology-inspired repair strategies inform polymer, composite, and infrastructure materials that can respond to microscopic damage.","concept_ids":["fatigue_failure","self_healing_materials","microvascular_repair","resilient_design"],"chapter_title":"Resilience and Material Repair"},{"type":"section","course":"bio-inspired","chapter":"08","section":"01","title":"Module 8: Swarm Intelligence & Autonomous Robotics","description":"Understanding how ant colonies and bird flocks execute highly complex global tasks using simple local rules, and how this applies to autonomous drone swarms.","concept_ids":[],"chapter_title":"Swarm Intelligence and Distributed Systems"},{"type":"section","course":"inst-design","chapter":"01","section":"01","title":"Module 1: What is Instructional Design?","description":"Introduce instructional design as the deliberate planning of learning experiences, then anchor the field in the major learning-theory traditions that shape design decisions.","concept_ids":["instructional_design","learning_theories","behaviorism","cognitivism","constructivism"],"chapter_title":"Introduction to Instructional Design"},{"type":"section","course":"inst-design","chapter":"01","section":"02","title":"Module 2: Cognitive Load & Schema","description":"Examine how working memory limits, schema development, and instructional presentation choices interact to support or overload learners.","concept_ids":["cognitive_load","schema_theory","intrinsic_load","extraneous_load","germane_load"],"chapter_title":"Introduction to Instructional Design"},{"type":"section","course":"inst-design","chapter":"01","section":"03","title":"Module 3: Constructivism & Active Learning","description":"Use constructivist theory, the Zone of Proximal Development, and scaffolding principles to design more active and meaning-rich learning experiences.","concept_ids":["constructivism","zone_of_proximal_development","scaffolding","active_learning"],"chapter_title":"Introduction to Instructional Design"},{"type":"section","course":"inst-design","chapter":"02","section":"01","title":"Module 1: Analysis & Design Phases","description":"Trace how instructional designers move from learner and context analysis into concrete learning goals, sequencing, and blueprint-level design decisions.","concept_ids":["analysis_phase","design_phase","learner_analysis","task_analysis","instructional_objectives"],"chapter_title":"The ADDIE Framework"},{"type":"section","course":"inst-design","chapter":"02","section":"02","title":"Module 2: Development & Implementation Phases","description":"Show how design plans become instructional assets and how those assets are launched, facilitated, and supported during implementation.","concept_ids":["development_phase","implementation_phase","storyboarding","instructional_assets","facilitation_logistics"],"chapter_title":"The ADDIE Framework"},{"type":"section","course":"inst-design","chapter":"02","section":"03","title":"Module 3: The Evaluation Phase","description":"Use formative and summative evidence to judge whether instruction is working, and interpret Kirkpatrick's model as a layered evaluation framework.","concept_ids":["evaluation_phase","formative_evaluation","summative_evaluation","kirkpatrick_model","evidence_collection"],"chapter_title":"The ADDIE Framework"},{"type":"section","course":"inst-design","chapter":"02","section":"04","title":"Module 4: Beyond ADDIE — Comparing ID Process Models","description":"Compare Dick and Carey, the Kemp model, Smith and Ragan, SAM, and Agile/Lean ID, then learn when to choose each.","concept_ids":["id_models","dick_and_carey_model","kemp_model","smith_and_ragan_model","successive_approximation_model","agile_id"],"chapter_title":"The ADDIE Framework"},{"type":"section","course":"inst-design","chapter":"02","section":"05","title":"Module 5: Writing Learning Objectives (Bloom + ABCD)","description":"Translate vague stakeholder wishes into testable performances using Bloom's revised taxonomy for cognitive level and Mager's ABCD format for performance specificity.","concept_ids":["learning_objectives","blooms_revised_taxonomy","mager_abcd","constructive_alignment","performance_objective"],"chapter_title":"The ADDIE Framework"},{"type":"section","course":"inst-design","chapter":"02","section":"06","title":"Module 6: Gagné's Nine Events of Instruction","description":"Use Gagné's information-processing-grounded nine events as a diagnostic frame for the internal architecture of a single lesson.","concept_ids":["gagne_nine_events","information_processing_model","lesson_architecture","retention_and_transfer","instructional_diagnosis"],"chapter_title":"The ADDIE Framework"},{"type":"section","course":"inst-design","chapter":"02","section":"07","title":"Module 7: Assessment Design — Validity, Reliability, and Item Construction","description":"Design defensible assessments by aligning to objectives, ensuring validity and reliability, writing diagnostic distractors, and using calibrated rubrics for open-ended performance.","concept_ids":["assessment_design","validity","reliability","constructive_alignment_audit","item_construction","rubric_design"],"chapter_title":"The ADDIE Framework"},{"type":"section","course":"inst-design","chapter":"02","section":"08","title":"Module 8: Online &amp; Distance Learning Design","description":"Design defensible online courses using Moore's three types of interaction and the Community of Inquiry model, and choose modes (asynchronous, synchronous, hybrid) deliberately.","concept_ids":["online_learning_design","moore_three_interactions","community_of_inquiry","cognitive_presence","social_presence","teaching_presence"],"chapter_title":"The ADDIE Framework"},{"type":"section","course":"inst-design","chapter":"03","section":"01","title":"Module 3: Multimedia Learning Principles","description":"Understanding how the brain processes multimedia information through Mayer's 12 Principles. Avoiding cognitive overload when combining text, audio, and graphics.","concept_ids":["multimedia_learning","mayer_principles","dual_channel_processing","cognitive_overload","modality_effect"],"chapter_title":"Multimedia and Message Design"},{"type":"section","course":"inst-design","chapter":"04","section":"01","title":"Module 4: Motivation, Engagement & The ARCS Model","description":"Understanding how to generate intrinsic and extrinsic motivation in adult learners using Keller's ARCS model and Game-Based Learning mechanics.","concept_ids":["arcs_model","learner_motivation","engagement_design","intrinsic_motivation","game_based_learning"],"chapter_title":"Motivation and Learner-Centered Design"},{"type":"section","course":"inst-design","chapter":"05","section":"01","title":"Module 5: Adult Learning Theory (Andragogy)","description":"Contrasting Pedagogy (teaching children) with Andragogy (teaching adults), focusing on self-direction, prior experience, and problem-centered learning.","concept_ids":["andragogy","adult_learning","self_directed_learning","problem_centered_learning"],"chapter_title":"Motivation and Learner-Centered Design"},{"type":"section","course":"inst-design","chapter":"06","section":"01","title":"Module 6: Collaborative & Social Learning","description":"Exploring Vygotsky's Zone of Proximal Development (ZPD) and Communities of Practice in modern, networked learning environments.","concept_ids":["collaborative_learning","social_learning","zone_of_proximal_development","communities_of_practice","peer_scaffolding"],"chapter_title":"Motivation and Learner-Centered Design"},{"type":"section","course":"inst-design","chapter":"07","section":"01","title":"Module 7: Universal Design for Learning (UDL)","description":"Designing accessible learning experiences that accommodate neurodiversity and physical disabilities by providing multiple means of representation, action, and engagement.","concept_ids":["universal_design_for_learning","multiple_means_of_representation","multiple_means_of_action_and_expression","multiple_means_of_engagement","inclusive_design"],"chapter_title":"Accessibility and Inclusive Learning Design"},{"type":"section","course":"inst-design","chapter":"08","section":"01","title":"Module 8: Emerging Technologies in Learning","description":"Analyzing the impact of Artificial Intelligence Tutors, Large Language Models (LLMs), and VR/AR on the future architecture of learning.","concept_ids":["ai_tutors","large_language_models","extended_reality_learning","future_learning_ecosystems","human_ai_orchestration"],"chapter_title":"Future Learning Systems"},{"type":"section","course":"ai-ethics","chapter":"01","section":"01","title":"Module 1: Why AI Needs Ethics","description":"Understand what makes AI ethics distinct from general engineering ethics: scale, encoded decisions, invisible stakeholders, and the shift toward generative and autonomous systems.","concept_ids":["ai_ethics_foundations","scale_argument","encoded_decisions","ai_stakeholders","generative_systems_ethics","ethics_vs_law"],"chapter_title":"Foundations of AI Ethics"},{"type":"section","course":"ai-ethics","chapter":"01","section":"02","title":"Module 2: Applying Ethics Frameworks to AI","description":"Triangulate AI deployment decisions across consequentialism, deontology, virtue ethics, and care ethics; recognize where the frameworks converge (permission to proceed) and where they diverge (the live decision).","concept_ids":["consequentialism","deontology","virtue_ethics","care_ethics","ethical_triangulation"],"chapter_title":"Foundations of AI Ethics"},{"type":"section","course":"ai-ethics","chapter":"02","section":"01","title":"Module 1: Algorithmic Bias and Where It Comes From","description":"Disentangle the six sources of algorithmic bias (historical, representation, measurement, aggregation, evaluation, deployment feedback) and match each to its appropriate intervention.","concept_ids":["algorithmic_bias","historical_bias","representation_bias","measurement_bias","aggregation_bias","evaluation_bias","deployment_feedback_bias"],"chapter_title":"Bias and Fairness"},{"type":"section","course":"ai-ethics","chapter":"02","section":"02","title":"Module 2: Fairness Definitions and Their Impossibilities","description":"Compare demographic parity, equalized odds, calibration within groups, individual fairness, and counterfactual fairness, and reason about the impossibility theorem that links them when base rates differ.","concept_ids":["fairness_definitions","demographic_parity","equalized_odds","calibration_within_groups","fairness_impossibility","individual_fairness","counterfactual_fairness"],"chapter_title":"Bias and Fairness"},{"type":"section","course":"ai-ethics","chapter":"03","section":"01","title":"Module 1: Black Boxes, Interpretability, and the Right to Explanation","description":"Distinguish interpretability from explainability, evaluate post-hoc explanation methods, and meet the legal-and-ethical floor for meaningful explanations under GDPR, EU AI Act, and FCRA.","concept_ids":["interpretability","explainability","post_hoc_explanation","counterfactual_explanation","right_to_explanation","gdpr_article_22"],"chapter_title":"Transparency and Accountability"},{"type":"section","course":"ai-ethics","chapter":"03","section":"02","title":"Module 2: Accountability and Liability","description":"Address the responsibility gap by distributing accountability across developer, deployer, operator, and user; recognize when human-in-the-loop is structural fig leaf; and apply engineering practices (audit trails, model cards, versioning, incident response) that make accountability real.","concept_ids":["responsibility_gap","accountability_distribution","human_in_the_loop","model_cards","audit_trails","incident_response"],"chapter_title":"Transparency and Accountability"},{"type":"section","course":"ai-ethics","chapter":"04","section":"01","title":"Module 1: Privacy Threats from AI Systems","description":"Identify four distinct privacy threats (re-identification, inference attacks, training-data leakage, surveillance-by-aggregation) and match each to its appropriate countermeasure (k-anonymity, differential privacy, federated learning, data minimization).","concept_ids":["re_identification","membership_inference","training_data_leakage","differential_privacy","federated_learning","data_minimization"],"chapter_title":"Privacy, Consent, and Data Governance"},{"type":"section","course":"ai-ethics","chapter":"04","section":"02","title":"Module 2: Consent in Algorithmic Contexts","description":"Apply the four conditions of informed consent (disclosure, comprehension, voluntariness, competence) to AI deployments, recognize dark patterns that defeat consent, and design defensible consent flows that meet GDPR and DSA standards.","concept_ids":["informed_consent","dark_patterns","consent_flow_design","voluntariness","withdrawal_rights","surrogate_consent"],"chapter_title":"Privacy, Consent, and Data Governance"},{"type":"section","course":"ai-ethics","chapter":"05","section":"01","title":"Module 1: AI Safety and Alignment Foundations","description":"Distinguish near-term safety from long-term alignment, recognize specification gaming and reward hacking, and identify five classes of intervention (better specification, interpretability, oversight, capability control, governance).","concept_ids":["ai_safety","ai_alignment","specification_gaming","reward_hacking","goodharts_law","distributional_shift","capability_vs_alignment"],"chapter_title":"Safety, Alignment, and Governance"},{"type":"section","course":"ai-ethics","chapter":"05","section":"02","title":"Module 2: Governance Frameworks — NIST AI RMF, EU AI Act, UNESCO","description":"Compare the three dominant AI governance frameworks (NIST AI RMF, EU AI Act, UNESCO Recommendation) and ISO/IEC 42001, identify where they overlap and disagree, and apply a practitioner workflow that uses each in combination.","concept_ids":["nist_ai_rmf","eu_ai_act","unesco_ai_ethics","iso_42001","ai_governance","risk_tiered_regulation"],"chapter_title":"Safety, Alignment, and Governance"},{"type":"section","course":"ai-ethics","chapter":"06","section":"01","title":"Module 1: Educational Data, FERPA, COPPA, and IDEA","description":"Map the three legal frameworks that govern AI deployments in education (FERPA, COPPA, IDEA) and the four ethical concerns the law only partly addresses (surveillance, equity, dark patterns, teacher de-skilling).","concept_ids":["education_ai_ethics","ferpa","coppa","idea_act","educational_surveillance","ai_equity_in_education","dark_patterns_in_education"],"chapter_title":"AI in Education Ethics"},{"type":"section","course":"ai-ethics","chapter":"06","section":"02","title":"Module 2: Surveillance, Dark Patterns, and Learner Autonomy","description":"Distinguish monitoring from surveillance, identify dark patterns specific to learner-facing AI (variable-ratio rewards, loss aversion, manufactured peer presence), and use self-determination theory to anchor a learner-autonomy-respecting design.","concept_ids":["educational_surveillance","learner_autonomy","self_determination_theory","engagement_dark_patterns","manufactured_social_presence"],"chapter_title":"AI in Education Ethics"},{"type":"section","course":"ail606-supplement","chapter":